Completion:
We are given a problem involving a **frog that jumps between the vertices of a triangle $ ABC $, moving each time to one of the neighboring vertices**. The question is:  
> **In how many ways can the frog return to vertex $ A $ from vertex $ A $ in $ n $ jumps?**

We are asked to **determine the number of ways that the frog can return to vertex $ A $ from vertex $ A $ in exactly $ n $ jumps**, where the frog moves **each time to one of the neighboring vertices**.

---

### **Step 1: Understanding the Structure of the Problem**

We are given a **triangle $ ABC $**, meaning that the **vertices of the triangle are labeled as $ A, B, C $**. Since the triangle is **a simple 3-vertex graph**, we can **model this as a graph** where:

- The **vertices are the 3 distinct vertices of the triangle**, that is, $ A, B, C $, and the **edges are the connections between the vertices** that are **"neighboring"**, i.e., **adjacent in the triangle**.

So, we are told:

- The **frog starts at vertex $ A $**.
- The **frog makes **exactly** $ n $ **moves**, where **in each move, the frog must move to a **different vertex (i.e., a neighboring vertex)**.
- **In every move, the frog is forced to move to one of the **neighboring vertices**, i.e., the **vertices that are adjacent to its current position**, but **cannot stay in the same vertex** (as per the phrasing: "moves each time to one of the neighboring vertices").

So, **the frog must move **from one vertex to another, and in each move, the frog must move to **a **different vertex** (i.e., must move to **a neighboring vertex**, **but not stay in the same vertex**) — but the **frog is **not allowed to stay in the same vertex** in any move.**

So, in **every jump, the frog moves to **a different vertex**, and **in every move, the frog is required to move to **a neighboring vertex** (i.e., a **vertex that is adjacent to the current vertex**).

So, the **frog must make a **sequence of $ n $ **moves**, such that:

- The **frog starts at vertex $ A $**
- The **frog makes **exactly** $ n $ **moves**
- In **each move, the frog moves from its current position to **one of the neighboring vertices (i.e., a **vertex that is adjacent to its current position)**
- In **each move, the frog **cannot stay at the same vertex** (i.e., the **frog must move to a **different vertex**)

So, **the frog is moving from one vertex to another, and in each move, the frog **must move to **a different vertex**, and **must move to a **neighboring vertex** (i.e., the frog must move to a **vertex that is adjacent to its current position), but **it is **not allowed to stay in the same vertex.**

